from pathlib import Path
import re
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
s=(P.parent/'research-20261014/paper.tex').read_text()
def para(start,new):
 global s
 a=s.index(start);b=s.find('\n\n',a)
 s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]
para('Requirements, source code, and tests evolve',r'''Requirements, code, and tests evolve at different rates. A renamed endpoint may leave stale client calls; a revised requirement may leave implementation and tests unchanged. Traceability identifies artifact relationships, whereas consistency assessment asks whether related artifacts agree at a particular revision. A plausible trace link is evidence for investigation, not proof of conformance.''')
para('Automated traceability research spans',r'''Lexical retrieval, learned representations, and language models support trace-link recovery \cite{slr}, but similarity and static connectivity alone do not establish conformance. RAG supplies external evidence to a generator \cite{rag}; LiSSA already applies it to multiple traceability tasks \cite{lissa}. We investigate how revision-specific evidence and bounded tools support review while preserving uncertainty about semantic correctness.''')
para('This paper contributes:',r'''This paper contributes a version-aware implementation and an auditable empirical separation of retrieval, evidence provenance, impact coverage, and consistency decisions. Four retrieval datasets, 12 controlled changes, and a recorded investigation expose mixed ranking effects and unresolved consistency detection. No new retrieval algorithm, novelty priority, or superior model is claimed.''')
para(r'\textit{RAG and LLM filtering.}',r'''\textit{RAG, structure, and decision scope.} LiSSA evaluates generic link recovery across artifact types \cite{lissa}; Hey \emph{et al.} study inter-requirements RAG \cite{reqrag}; and Fuch\ss{} \emph{et al.} investigate LLM candidate filtering \cite{ensemble}. Vigilant's ranking experiment only reorders five retrieved requirement--code candidates. Its separate change workflow attaches Git revisions, checked source spans, structural neighbors, rule hypotheses, and an explicit consistency decision to a review record. Thus, ranking evaluation and revision-specific consistency evaluation have different units and labels; published scores are not treated as comparable baselines.''')
para(r'\textit{Structure, consistency, and agents.}',r'''Architecture-documentation inconsistency detection \cite{archcons} and TransArC's architecture-mediated documentation--code links \cite{transarc} establish relevant structural precedents. Vigilant uses source-level calls, imports, tests, and literal API relations to propose revision-local impacts, without asserting that these paths prove conformance. SWE-agent studies agent--computer interfaces \cite{sweagent}; Vigilant instead executes a fixed tool sequence. The contribution is this implemented separation of evidence and decisions, assessed with negative results, rather than integration alone as a novelty claim.''')
para(r'Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows',r'''Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows the investigation data flow. Base and head graphs are composed for impact exploration. The implementation traverses an undirected view for at most two hops, excluding default reverse-containment expansion. The traversal proposes candidates from either revision; reachability does not confirm a defect.''')
s=s.replace('graph edges and retrieved links remain evidence candidates.','the dashed arrow adds rule-targeted evidence; relationships remain candidates.')
para('Lexical retrieval uses normalized tokens',r'''Lexical retrieval uses normalized tokens, including split identifiers, with TF-IDF or BM25. Dense retrieval uses \texttt{all-MiniLM-L6-v2}, producing 384-dimensional vectors \cite{minilm}. Target text is divided into chunks of 180 normalized words, with at most eight chunks. Chunk vectors are averaged and normalized. Embeddings use CPU float32, batch size 24, four threads, and a model/text-keyed cache. Chunk caps can omit late file content.''')
para('The traceability experiment instead presents',r'''The traceability experiment instead presents a requirement prefix of at most 900 characters and five candidate prefixes of at most 1,100 characters each. Qwen labels candidates \texttt{SUPPORTED}, \texttt{UNSUPPORTED}, or \texttt{UNCERTAIN} using evidence aliases. Retrieval supplies evidence; the generated labels only control reordering. Supported candidates move ahead within the original top five; remaining order is stable. The fixed top-five and top-ten sets make Recall@5/@10 invariant. We call this operation RAG-assisted LLM reranking, distinct from agentic investigation.''')
para('The measured generator is',r'''The measured generator is \texttt{Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct} \cite{qwen}, executed locally on CPU in bfloat16 with four threads. Decoding is greedy, inputs exceeding 3,072 tokens are rejected, output at 220 new tokens, and a grammar constrains JSON generation. Neither the generator nor encoder is fine-tuned. Consistency labels must agree across the decision fields. A non-uncertain judgment requires positive confidence and a supplied evidence citation; unknown evidence/artifact aliases cause an uncertain fallback. These syntactic conditions do not establish entailment. \texttt{SUPPORTED/UNSUPPORTED} belong to the separate trace-candidate schema, not the consistency schema.''')
para('The bounded orchestration has 12 stages:',r'''The 12 prescribed stages resolve revisions, import snapshots, compute differences, build base and head graphs, collect impact candidates, recover trace candidates, retrieve evidence, apply rules, verify excerpts, request a model decision, and persist the run. Role labels organize responsibilities; the model does not choose tools autonomously. Rules flag removed endpoints, changed code with unchanged tests, deleted last-known tests, or obsolete client routes. These findings remain review hypotheses, stored separately from model status, confidence, citations, and uncertainty. Tool completion does not imply a resolved investigation.''')
para('The consistency study uses 12 authored',r'''The consistency configuration study uses 12 authored Git mutations across RTMX, Flask, and a full-stack FastAPI/React repository with expected labels: six potential inconsistencies, three uncertain cases, two broken traces, and one missing-test case. Five configurations each make 12 actual calls: question only; hybrid evidence; hybrid plus graph; additional bounded inspection; and temporal evidence plus rules. This is a configuration comparison, not an isolated ablation: inputs and budgets differ, and graph-assisted contexts have authored baseline trace relations. Affected-artifact oracles are incomplete; outside-oracle endpoints are unjudged. Saved rules-only findings are reported separately in the supplement, without forcing them into a single-label accuracy metric.''')
s=s.replace('No statistical significance test is claimed.',r'''A post-hoc paired bootstrap resamples per-query AP differences within each dataset (20,000 replicates; seeds 20261015--20261018), yielding percentile 95\% intervals conditional on the frozen model and prompt. These assume exchangeable queries, may understate correlation, and are not project-generalization intervals or multiplicity-adjusted tests.''')
s=s.replace('Absolute MAP differences produced by top-five RAG reranking. Positive values favor reranking; negative values favor the hybrid baseline. No confidence intervals were estimated.',r'MAP differences produced by LLM reranking. Points show signed changes; bars show descriptive paired-query bootstrap 95\% intervals. Positive values favor reranking.')
s=s.replace('RAG reranking','RAG-assisted LLM reranking')
needle='All 251 responses pass the recorded full-schema checks'
pos=s.index(needle)
s=s[:pos]+r'''The 95\% intervals are $[0.0002,0.0073]$, $[-0.0244,0.0177]$, $[-0.0119,0.0169]$, and $[-0.1485,0.0212]$. Only SMOS excludes zero under this query-level resampling; no cross-project superiority follows. '''+s[pos:]
para('All five consistency configurations return',r'''Every configuration predicts \texttt{UNCERTAIN} in 12/12 cases, matching the always-uncertain baseline: 25\% authored-label agreement, macro-F1 0.10, and 0\% non-uncertain coverage. For each variant, the confusion matrix has only its UNCERTAIN column nonzero: 6 potential inconsistencies, 2 broken traces, 1 missing test, and 3 uncertain cases. UNCERTAIN precision/recall/F1 are 0.25/1.00/0.40; the other classes have zero recall and F1. Their undefined precision is assigned zero for macro averaging. Matrices and per-class metrics are provided separately; repeated configurations are not 60 independent cases.

All 60 raw outputs already select UNCERTAIN with confidence zero; five additionally fail citation-alias validation. The validator therefore does not explain the universal abstention. The prompt includes an uncertain example, and 46 outputs give ``Evidence is incomplete'' as their entire reason. Prompt anchoring and restricted excerpts are plausible explanations, not isolated causal findings. Citation validity is 12/12, 11/12, 11/12, 11/12, and 10/12 across configurations. Consistency detection remains unresolved.''')
s=s.replace('Mean generation durations','Observed mean generation durations')
para('RQ1 has a project-dependent answer:',r'''RQ1 is dataset-dependent: lexical baselines remain competitive, graph relevance changes ranking trade-offs, and a constrained generation stage can worsen retrieved order. The bootstrap describes variation across the observed queries, not across models, prompts, or future projects. Repeating deterministic calls would not supply that missing variation.''')
para('For RQ2, richer evidence improves',r'''For RQ2, evidence coverage rises while semantic label agreement remains at the abstention baseline. Rules-only outputs are multi-label hypothesis sets: their inclusion of authored labels is not a comparable single-label accuracy result or independent correctness evidence. A controlled follow-up must fix cases, prompt budget, model, and decision policy while varying one evidence source, with independently adjudicated labels. The accompanying protocol specifies these controls without claiming an unrun experiment.''')
para('For RQ3, the recorded investigation',r'''For RQ3, three outcomes remain distinct: evidence validity checks a quote against a revision; impact coverage measures recovery of known endpoints; semantic correctness requires the conclusion to follow from evidence. The pipeline demonstrates the first and partial coverage, not the third. Continuous operation is an implemented change trigger, with no longitudinal validation. Local execution avoids per-call API charges but consumes hardware and time; the unevaluated OmniRoute extension establishes neither accuracy nor universally free operation.''')
s=s.replace('and no significance analysis.','Bootstrap intervals are post-hoc and conditional on correlated within-project queries; no confirmatory significance claim is made.')
s=s.replace('There is one saved configuration per method, no independent semantic annotation, Bootstrap','There is one saved configuration per method and no independent semantic annotation. Bootstrap')
s=s.replace('The present evidence supports an auditable review framework and a reproducible evaluation, not an automatic correctness guarantee.','Consistency assurance is an objective, not a validated capability. The present evidence supports an auditable review framework and a reproducible evaluation.')
s=s.replace('The contribution is an auditable implementation and a scoped empirical assessment that separates retrieval performance, evidence validity, and consistency assurance rather than treating them as interchangeable outcomes.','Consistency detection remains unresolved. The contribution is an auditable implementation and empirical separation of ranking, evidence provenance, impact coverage, and semantic correctness.')
s=s.replace(r'\emph{Found. Trends Inf. Retr.}, 2009',r'\emph{Found. Trends Inf. Retr.}, vol. 3, no. 4, pp. 333--389, 2009')
for pin in ['1110a243fdf4','989aa7980e4c','c2ced5349577']:s=s.replace(pin+'. [Online]',pin+', accessed Oct. 15, 2026. [Online]')
(P/'paper.tex').write_text(s)
